WEBVTT

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.215
Training and inference efficiency should be measured as useful output per dollar,

00:00:04.215 --> 00:00:06.360
not simply as maximum hardware activity.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.300
A busy GPU is not automatically cost-effective if the work is low value,

00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:13.100
the run is misconfigured, or the result is not usable.

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.840
For training, batch size, model size, data loading, checkpoint frequency,

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.770
precision choices, runtime settings,

00:00:18.770 --> 00:00:21.295
and distributed coordination can all affect utilization.

00:00:21.295 --> 00:00:23.000
The goal is not to tune endlessly.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.970
The goal is to find the point where the workload produces reliable results with reasonable

00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.500
cost and operational complexity.

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.260
For inference, demand patterns matter.

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.357
Caching can reduce repeated work when responses or intermediate results are reusable.

00:00:34.357 --> 00:00:36.664
Batching can improve throughput for some services.

00:00:36.664 --> 00:00:39.110
Autoscaling can help when it follows real demand,

00:00:39.110 --> 00:00:43.055
but it can waste money if it reacts to noisy signals or keeps too much capacity warm.

00:00:43.055 --> 00:00:45.035
Smaller models, optimized deployments,

00:00:45.035 --> 00:00:48.764
or different serving patterns may meet the same business need at lower cost.

00:00:48.764 --> 00:00:52.250
Those choices should be evaluated against quality, latency, safety,

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.990
and reliability requirements, not only raw spend.

00:00:54.990 --> 00:00:57.800
Efficiency work is most useful when it is connected to measurement.

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.450
Teams should know the cost per run, cost per batch, cost per evaluation,

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.690
or cost per useful request where that is practical.

00:01:03.690 --> 00:01:06.295
That turns optimization from guesswork into engineering.

00:01:06.295 --> 00:01:09.360
The safest efficiency program makes small, measurable changes.

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.045
Adjust one setting, compare results,

00:01:11.045 --> 00:01:13.800
and keep the version that meets requirements with less waste.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.380
That approach avoids trading away reliability for a short-term cost drop.
